2026-10-24 T-317F: Capture normalized tool failures centrally in a machine-local SQLite ledger, with allowlisted summaries instead of raw payloads. Reuse authenticated API and Conductor tools for grouped triage and explicit remediation; never disable a tool from one failure. Keep diagnostics local because tool installations and runtime conditions differ per machine. Global prompt policy covers every role and harness; hidden inner calls require safe agent reporting. No live server restart or unsupported config edit. Source thread 317ffed2-e4bf-4748-812d-488b6dc0662a.
2026-10-28 T-TKOV: Take over a thread from another synced Mac as a request on top of Hand off: the asking Mac writes handoff.request.<thread>, the Mac that holds the thread answers with the usual offer, and the asking Mac takes it with the existing pickup. The holder between turns is handoff.holder.<thread>, written only by the Mac a thread moves to (a turn starting there, or a pickup), not a thread column, because the thread row is last-writer-wins and a rename on the other Mac would overwrite it. The holder answers only as holder and only onto a settled offer, so a stale Mac never gives an old session away; only the holder may Hand off. The asking Mac alone ends a request (cancel, one hour, or the thread moving), so no Mac compares clocks. A sleeping holder makes the request wait; forced takeover, checkpoints and crew threads stay out. Source threads 0cdcc478 (plan) and fdf85080-5a5d-427a-8f8f-aaa2cc390fe7.
2026-10-30 T-399: One execution owner per thread: an `assignments` row (machine, generation, routed) that every turn, pickup and routing claims, synced by generation so a late copy of an old owner never wins. The live registry derives from it; `run_machine` stays a status-write tag; `handoff.holder` and `runon.thread` are kept only for older builds. The check moved to the delivery boundary: `turnBlocked` for Ben's replies, `wakeBlocked` for Omni's own wakes (which never move a thread), applied in the runner's `wake`. Generation fencing stops a stale turn after sync. A disconnected machine may start work on what it holds and nothing else; no lease expires on its own. "Remote provider" (Hermes, Cursor Cloud: no local files) is named apart from "remote machine" (another synced Mac). Issue #399.
